{"kind":"request","text":"AE-1 closed-result ordering clarification. A declared package scope proves its own install in preparePolicies before any provider call; a permanent refusal there records application-policy-unavailable/proofReason, preserves the old row and makes zero provider calls. Native Rules/Directory journals have native policies; their own package-install entry selects additional installed package policy in sources.prepare(candidate), after existing target/format or current-parent provider reads. A refusal stops before further provider work, but those earlier reads may have happened. Does 12115613 require zero calls also for that native control-journal candidate? If yes, please rule on moving bounded native candidate policy discovery before provider work, including whether to preflight the selected cutoff or whole native prefix. A blanket whole-prefix scan would change cost and source scheduling; I have not added it. ARC-1 separately owns the future per-entry supported-cohort preflight, so AE-1 should not invent that predicate. Current draft documents only zero calls for a scope's own preparation and no calls after any refusal.","body":{"conditions":"Clarify zero-provider ordering for native control-journal candidate package proofs before builder widens archive pass planning. Source only.","no_git_artifact":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:12115613a7cecf425c34bdbd1e437a884003f2dc, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:59767cd8d1c09c50c9bfda8cd440bed3cda6142f stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b5750e6e82650d29470d64f83b0dc3a50115b466","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}